# SYNOPSIS

```text
userboot.next=bin/launcher launcher.until=dt-test
```

# DESCRIPTION

dt-test is the last service of the devicetree image, `bootfs-devicetree.img` (rv64), which holds devmgr(8) with its drivers and the services its manifest mounts: kbdfs(4), mouse(4), the console and proc(4).
It is for a machine whose firmware gives only a devicetree, where the loader makes the ACPI tables and the boot data carries the tree (intro(8)): QEMU's `virt` with ACPI off, and a board such as the Orange Pi RV2 before its drivers run.
The launcher runs until dt-test ends and exits with its code (launcher(8)).

It waits up to 30 seconds for devmgr to bind its drivers, then checks that devmgr's `devicetree` lists the tree's devices, that each has its `dt-` line in devmgr's `status`, either `unbound` or `running`, that one device has both registers and an interrupt (a range translated through its buses and an interrupt resolved to the kernel's controller), and that each device a link names as its provider runs its driver, where it has one.
Where a provider runs (on the Orange Pi RV2, k1-syscon(3) for the K1's APBC and APMU), it writes `clock <id> on` to that provider's `ctl` for the first clock a device names on it, and checks that the provider's `status` then reads the clock `on` (or `ungated`); without one it prints `dt-test: no provider runs: no clock to turn on`.
It prints devmgr's `devicetree` and `status`, a line each, then a line for each check, `dt-test: ok:` or `dt-test: FAILED:` and what it checks; its last line is `dt-test: passed` or `dt-test: FAILED, <n> checks`.
It exits 0 if every check passed.

